xplicitly not recommending. Actually maybe safer to say: \"This guide does not ask for a per-job key; the decision is the Owner's and it is made.\" I'll phrase: \"The Owner weighed a per-job key with a provider-side ceiling and chose the shared key; this guide records that decision and does not reopen it.\" That's clear.\n\nNow, item (d): every rule paired with a real story. Let me enumerate the rules I state and ensure each has a story from the drafts:\n\nRules from (c):\n1. finish_reason length + empty content = own failure class, stop at first one → story: VizH 12-record call, 5 failures at 131,072 tokens, ~$0.59/call, ~$3 before change; plus ours: 24k cap and 800-token GLM.\n2. No throttling: endpoint max output, no reasoning limit → story: 24k cap (19.9k thinking, nothing usable); 800-token GLM instrument (all three calls ~798 tokens reasoning, batch void, amendment).\n3. Size each task → story: batching 12 records into four calls of three fixed it.\n4. finish_reason logged on every call → story: guarded harness logs finish (stop, 21,753/26,185 tokens); without it you guess. Hmm, need a \"real story\". The draft says \"Ours: the guarded harness already logs finish (doctrine-pointer iter 1: stop, 21,753 / 26,185 tokens). Keep it that way, and make the sizing part of the bar, not luck.\" That's a story-ish. Also the starved calls showing in the log. I'll pair it with the 12-record story: \"the five failed calls only made sense because the log carried finish=length with token counts; without the logged finish_reason nobody could tell a starved call from a refused one.\" Hmm, is that inventing? The drafts say \"have the harness log finish_reason along with the reasoning and content token counts for every call. That way a starved call shows up in the log and nobody has to guess.\" I'll frame it as: the reason we log it is the 12-record failure — the diagnosis came from the logged finish_reason and token split. That's a reasonable reading, not invented.\n\nActually careful: \"Do not invent an incident\". I'll keep close to the drafts.\n\n5. Cap counts E-DI iterations → story (required by feedback).\n6. Handoffs name the command that writes files → story: MAKER.md iter 1 went from `extract_files.py --check` (writes nothing) straight to \"commit\"; the Maker spotted it and ran the command without `--check`.\n7. Check handoffs against standing rules → story: a helper-written iter-1 MAKER.md capped Maker retries at one and committed as the Owner; both contradict standing rules; caught and overridden in the relay.\n8. Live test of each model instrument is a freeze gate → story: VizH dry-ran deterministic instruments (64/64) but never ran the records splitter live; five paid calls failed after freeze. Ours: 800-token GLM. And the job that did it right: both pinned providers live-tested before freeze, output committed.\n9. Write-once evidence → story: VizH's working rule (no specific incident) + ours: the emitter rewrites evidence logs in place, filling send/receive times from the server clock, and running tests changes two committed fixture logs (sheet item 28). Careful: \"VizH's working rule. The review gives it without a specific incident.\" J4 requires a real story for every rule. So I must pair write-once with the concrete incident: the emitter rewriting logs in place / tests changing committed fixture logs. Good, that's real from the drafts. I'll lead with that one and mention the reviewer's practice as corroboration (not as the story).\n10. Relayed approvals are not approvals → story: gen 5's report didn't include the Owner's yes; the close line could only say the go-ahead was in the Maker's chat and the Orchestrator didn't witness it. Also the seats' go-aheads logged as relays. Also VizH: in their jobs the Owner runs the logging scripts himself. Good.\n11. No /tmp → story: the reboot at ~08:36 EDT during Maker gen 4 call 3; /tmp wiped including the test venv, scratch worktree vanished; the Maker had to rebuild. Plus TMPDIR story: gen-5 handoff moved named paths but tests still wrote to /tmp; one test script alone left 32 temp folders.\n\nOther rules from the drafts:\n12. Whole files between markers, not diffs → story: gen 2 four corrupt diffs; gen 3 whole files passed on call 2; VizH 9 of 10 diff attempts failed.\n13. Time bound as clock time → story: call-start cutoff vs job end confusion.\n14. Plan approvals against clock (and decision cap) → story: gen 3 waited overnight, job blew the time bound; decision cap 10 used 12, one unattributable.\n15. Spend enforced in harness → story: ceilings in a handoff are prose; shared key story (ledger and key total never reconciled; key's own limit stopped a call partway) — that's VizH's. Ours: doctrine-pointer spend matched by luck.\n16. Malformed input probe → story: tiebreak instrument didn't implement the spec rule for missing/malformed fields and settled a split the wrong way.\n17. Verify the Maker's report yourself → story: acceptance on a copy of the tree, count a step only if it ran (VizH). Hmm, is that a \"story\"? It's thin. Maybe pair with: in gen 5 call 2 the files crashed the suite... Actually better: pair \"verify\" with the base-commit run story (gen 5, FileNotFoundError, base b934b48 112/112). That's a real incident where verification mattered. I'll put \"verify\" and \"run the base commit first\" together with that story.\n18. Read the diff and say what kind of fix it is → story: gen 5 fixture-only fix (four ledger events carrying hand-typed times, emitter cross-checks relaxed).\n19. Writable copy of controls → story: read-only copy, fixtures copied with permissions, five checks failed, batch 1 void.\n20. Control order rotates → story: batch 4, n6/stdout.json order [subject, G-c1, G-p1], subject n6-b4-1 not -2.\n21. Subject prompt verbatim rebuild numbered listing → hmm, story? The draft has no explicit story. Maybe skip this rule to avoid a storyless rule. Or include with a story: \"a plain file dump will not match byte for byte, even when nothing is wrong\" — that's a reason. I'll skip it to keep J4 clean. Actually the drafts' own line: \"To check that the subject prompt was sent verbatim, rebuild it exactly as the harness does, numbered listing included.\" No story. Skip.\n22. Read key usage 2-3 minutes after last call → story: batch 4 waited 156 s and reconciled to the cent (fix for an earlier under-count). Good, that's a story.\n23. Box failure mid-run (reboot) → story: the reboot. This overlaps with /tmp. I'll include as part of records/robustness with the reboot story.\n24. Environment dependencies outside the repo into preconditions → story: the corpus symlink at a Mac path (don't write the path) that was a root-owned symlink; the reboot wiped it; tests couldn't run until recreated. Careful: no absolute paths. I'll say \"the test suite reads the doctrine corpus through `tools/pin.json` at a path outside the repository (a symlink on the box)\".\n25. Record tool/venv versions at job open → story: venv rebuilt after reboot, nothing recorded, identical output was the only evidence.\n26. Take time floors from the log, not a summary → story: floor 14:23:43Z from notes vs G-p1 at 14:24:26Z; moved to 14:24:49Z.\n27. Have the close ready before the Owner decides → story: disposition 10:44:00 EDT, close committed 28 s later, PR merged 57 s after.\n28. After the merge, later record changes on a new branch from the merge commit → story: the guide's final sync on a branch cut from main b8fef62.\n29. A merge changes the next job's starting point; re-read the pins → story: after the merge main's emitter is the fixed one.\n30. Separation gaps disclosure → story: our seating, instruments and pass rule in one seat, not disclosed in the separationGaps.\n31. Commit instruments and redacted logs beside the work → story: VizH's v0.9.7 record without log/evidence/instruments, public check NOT-CHECKABLE on two requirements.\n32. Log every Owner decision as it happens → story: decision cap 10, 12 used, one unattributable.\n33. Accept header → story: Cloudflare script injected only for HTML requests.\n34. Batch seal must not list itself — no story. Skip or fold into evaluator turn without stating as a rule? I could include it as a one-liner under evaluator turn without a story... but J4 says every rule stated must be paired with a story. To be safe, skip it. Hmm, but it's a real practice. I'll skip to keep J4 clean.\n\nAlso close-preparation rules: CL1-16 (script evaluator's script must not be the instrument's runner) — story: first dry run failed CL1-16. That's a story. Include? It's specific and useful. Maybe include briefly in a \"Preparing the close\" subsection with its story. It adds length; ~15KB target. Let's see.\n\nThe document should be ~15 KB. That's roughly 2,300-2,600 words. Let me be reasonably comprehensive but not bloated.\n\nLet me now check line 27 issue: I need the cap rule with its story. Structure in section 2:\n\n```\n- **Iteration** = one turn of the loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. **The cap counts E-DI iterations**, and nothing else. The Owner settled that on 2026-10-28 after a bar read the other way.\n  - *Story:* that bar said \"cap 3 generations.\" Generations 1 and 2 failed for plumbing reasons (a token cap, malformed diffs) before the Evaluator had scored anything. When the first real iteration then failed scoring, the bar said the attempts were gone, and the Owner had to amend the cap to allow a second real iteration. Nothing about the work had been judged twice; the count had simply been spent on plumbing.\n- **Maker-internal attempts** ... are not iterations ... limited by budget, the clock, and the tripwire.\n  - *Story:* same job, the other side of the same ledger: unlimited retries are how a broken delivery path hides behind the budget... \n```\n\nHmm, careful not to duplicate the same story twice in a confusing way. The feedback says: \"Keep the line-33 item and its story (it may refer back).\" So: the cap rule gets the story; the \"write the cap in one sentence\" item can say \"the same incident: ...\" and refer back.\n\nLet me write:\n\n**2.2 The cap counts iterations**\n- Rule: The cap counts E-DI iterations — one Maker produce, one Evaluator score, one pass-rule application. Maker-internal attempts (retries, follow-ups, regenerations inside one turn) are not iterations.\n- *Story:* bar said \"cap 3 generations\"; gens 1-2 died on plumbing (token cap, malformed diffs) before any scoring; iteration 1 failed scoring and the bar said the attempts were gone; the Owner had to amend the cap to allow a second real iteration.\n- Then a follow-on item: write the cap in one sentence, \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the repeat-failure tripwire.\" with a note: the story is the one above; also VizH's jobs hit the same thing with \"3 revisions\" counting sessions and round-trips.\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me draft the whole thing carefully.\n\nAlso need: \"finish_reason\" appears with \"length\" and \"empty content\" and \"own failure class\" — one place: \"Treat `finish_reason` `length` with empty content as its own failure class: stop at the first one, resize the request, and do not retry it as is.\"\n\nCareful: normalization drops backticks, so `finish_reason` → finish_reason (underscore dropped → \"finishreason\"). Wait! The normalize rule: \"drop the characters * _ ` and backslash\". So `finish_reason` becomes \"finishreason\"! Then the anchor \"finish_reason\" would NOT match!! Hmm. The anchor list says C1 needs \"finish_reason\". If underscores are dropped from the text, then \"finish_reason\" in the anchor must also be normalized the same way... The evaluator presumably normalizes both the text and the anchors the same way, so anchor \"finish_reason\" → \"finishreason\" and my text \"finish_reason\" → \"finishreason\" matches. Yes — normalization applied to both sides. So it's fine either way. But to be safe, does the evaluator normalize the anchors? \"Phrase matching works on the normalized text\" — ambiguous. Safest: include both \"finish_reason\" and a plain \"finish reason\"? If anchors are normalized and text normalized, \"finish_reason\" matches \"finishreason\". If anchors are not normalized and text is, then \"finish_reason\" anchor would never match any text. That would make the check impossible for everyone, so presumably anchors are normalized too. But to be extra safe, I could write \"finish_reason (finish reason)\" — hmm, that would normalize to \"finishreason finish reason\" which contains both \"finishreason\" and \"finish reason\". That's ugly but safe. Hmm.\n\nActually, a cleaner trick: write \"`finish_reason`\" in a sentence and also write \"the finish reason field\" somewhere? That's awkward. Let me think about what's likely: fg_rules.json says normalize applies to text; the checker likely normalizes both text and needles (standard practice). I'll include \"finish_reason\" as-is in several places, and also once write \"the finish reason\" in prose? Hmm.\n\nConsider: if anchors are NOT normalized, then anchor \"finish_reason\" requires literal \"finish_reason\" in the normalized text — but normalization strips underscores from the text, so the text would have \"finishreason\" and the literal anchor could never match. Therefore anchors must be normalized (or the checker normalizes needles). So writing \"finish_reason\" is fine. Good, no trick needed.\n\nSame for \"endpoint max\": normalized text keeps spaces; \"endpoint max\" must appear. \"endpoint maximum\" contains it. ✔\n\nNow, one more: \"cap counts\" + \"iterations\" — \"The cap counts E-DI iterations\" → normalized \"the cap counts e-di iterations\" — wait, hyphen kept? Normalization doesn't remove hyphens (only en/em dashes become '-'). So \"E-DI\" stays \"e-di\". \"cap counts\" appears. ✔\n\n\"no throttling\" — write literally \"No throttling.\" ✔\n\"reasoning limit\" ✔\n\"endpoint maximum\" ✔\n\"size each task\" ✔\n\"every call\" ✔\n\"command that writes\" ✔\n\"standing rules\" ✔\n\"live test\" ✔ and \"freeze gate\" ✔\n\"write-once\" ✔\n\"relayed approvals\" + \"not approvals\" — I'll write \"Relayed approvals are not approvals.\" ✔ (normalized: \"relayed approvals are not approvals\") contains \"relayed approvals\" and \"not approvals\". ✔\n\"/tmp\" ✔\n\"carried forward\" in a heading ✔ and in text ✔\n\"same failure class\" ✔ \"third time\" ✔ \"change the request\" ✔\n\"shared key\" ✔ \"spend guard\" ✔ \"upper bound\" ✔\n\"pre-read\" ✔ \"optional\" ✔ \"approval sheet\" ✔\n\nNow G7: avoid \"Steve\", \"Moitozo\", \"S2\". Note \"S2\" case-sensitive word boundary. I must not write \"S2\". Fine.\n\nAlso the deny regex `key[^\\n]{0,24}(fingerprint|sha256|sha-256|hash)[^\\n]{0,12}[0-9a-f]{8,}` — avoid writing \"key ... hash ... hex\". I'll avoid mentioning key fingerprints at all. Note: \"the full log's sha\" — that's fine (no \"key\" within 24 chars before). Let me check: \"with the full log's sha alongside\" — no \"key\". Fine.\n\nCareful: \"the key's usage ... sha256\"? I won't.\n\nAnother: `\\bsk-[a-z0-9_-]{16,}` — avoid.\n\nNow, let me also double check that I don't accidentally write \"OpenRouter key\" in a way that trips... \"OpenRouter\" is fine.\n\nHmm, also should I avoid the word \"openrouter\"? Not denied. The drafts mention it. I'll say \"the shared provider key\" to be safe and generic. Actually mentioning the provider isn't necessary.\n\nNow let me think about J3: nothing may contradict the Owner decisions, item 30(c) rules, and DOCTRINE.md. Things to be careful about:\n- Doctrine says the Maker never judges its own work; guide agrees.\n- Doctrine: \"the record and instrument committed beside it\" — guide agrees.\n- Doctrine: cap/budget/time supplied, never defaulted — guide agrees.\n- The guide must not claim to override the doctrine — status line says doctrine wins.\n- Don't say \"the cap counts generations\".\n- Don't recommend a per-job key.\n- Tripwire exactly 3 (not \"2 or 3\"). Write \"exactly three\": \"when the same failure class comes up a third time — exactly three, not two and not five\". Good, matches \"at exactly 3\".\n\nAlso: \"no throttling (endpoint max output, no reasoning limit)\".\n\nNow write the file. I'll aim for clean field-guide prose, headings, rules with *Story:* bullets.\n\nLet me draft.\n\n---\n\n# Running a job as Orchestrator: a field guide\n\nStatus: non-normative working document. It records practice that has worked and the failures that taught it. It is not part of the doctrine and not part of any custody record, and where it disagrees with the doctrine, the doctrine wins.\n\nStarted 2026-10-28 during the Plumbline job `2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps`, which closed accepted that morning, and revised the same day with a review from VizH, Architect on the evaluatordriven custody jobs v0.9.6 and v0.9.7, and with three Owner decisions given at 15:52 EDT (section 8). Reviewer passages are marked **[VizH]**; the three decisions are marked **[Owner]**.\n\nRead this before you open a job. Every rule below has a story behind it, from a real job or review where the rule was missing. The stories are here so you can reason from the why when you meet a case the rules do not cover.\n\nCareful: \"which closed accepted that morning\" — fine.\n\nThen:\n\n## 1. What you are, and what you are not\n\nYou are the Orchestrator... not the Maker, not the Evaluator.\n\nSeparation gaps paragraph with story.\n\nRelayed approvals paragraph with story.\n\n## 2. The words that matter\n\n- Bar\n- Frozen set\n- Iteration and the cap (with story)\n- The cap written in one sentence (refer back)\n- The repeat-failure tripwire (with story) [Owner]\n\n## 3. Before you freeze\n\n1. No throttling (story 1, story 2)\n2. Size each task, log finish_reason on every call (story)\n3. Live test each model instrument: a freeze gate (story)\n4. Whole files between markers; the runbook names the command that writes them (story)\n5. Time bound as a clock time (story)\n6. Plan approvals against the clock and against any decision cap (story)\n7. Enforce spend in the harness; shared key + spend guard + upper bound (story)\n8. Cross-family pre-read optional, decided per job on the approval sheet (story)\n9. Probe every checker with malformed input (story)\n\nFreeze gate checklist.\n\n## 4. Running the loop\n\nMaker turn:\n- preconditions\n- relay the shortfall unedited\n- verify the Maker's report; run the base commit before blaming the Maker (story)\n- finish_reason length empty content: own failure class (story)\n- read the diff and name the kind of fix (story)\n- ask the Maker to quote the go-ahead (story)\n- diff each filled handoff against the last (story)\n- check the handoff against the standing rules (story)\n- the handoff names the command that writes the files (story)\n\nEvaluator turn:\n- writable copy (story)\n- key usage lag (story)\n- subject or setup (story)\n- control order rotates (story)\n- real signal in a void batch (story)\n\nPass rule.\n\n## 5. Records\n- job record in the product repo; instruments and redacted logs beside it (story)\n- ledger append-only\n- write-once evidence (story)\n- amendments beside the frozen set\n- Owner decision log (story)\n- model-judge logs redacted\n- key by path only\n- git identity\n\n## 6. The box under you\n- reboot (story)\n- no /tmp (story)\n- TMPDIR (story)\n- environment dependencies in preconditions (story)\n- versions at job open (story)\n\n## 7. Working with the Owner\n- lead with the answer\n- apply a principle everywhere\n- own mistakes\n- accept header (story)\n\n## 8. Three Owner decisions, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT\na) tripwire (restate + stories)\nb) shared key with spend guard; upper bound; no per-job key\nc) pre-read optional per job\n\n## 9. Carried forward\n\nHmm, that's a lot. ~15KB. Let's estimate: each section ~1-2KB. Might reach 18-20KB. Let's trim: merge section 8 into sections 2/3/5 (the decisions are already stated there) — but a dedicated section makes the J3 reading clean. I'll keep section 8 but short, referencing the earlier statements rather than repeating fully. Actually repetition risks inconsistency. Let me structure: state each of the three decisions once, in its natural place, marked [Owner], with full story. Then a short section near the end: \"The three Owner decisions of 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT, in one place\" that lists them in one sentence each with a pointer. That's ~10 lines.\n\nHmm, but J3 wants to read the whole guide against the decisions; having them once clearly is enough. I'll do the pointer section briefly.\n\nLet me now consider trimming: drop \"Preparing the close\" details? The drafts have a lot of close material with stories. It's valuable but adds length. I'll include a compact \"Preparing the close\" subsection with 3 items: time floors from the log (story), close ready before the decision (story), after the merge a new branch (story), re-read pins (story), CL1-16 (story). That's 5 stories ~ 12 lines. Keep it tight.\n\nTotal budget: let's write and count roughly. I'll aim for around 15-17 KB. That's acceptable (\"expect about 15 KB\").\n\nLet me write the actual text now, carefully.\n\n---\n\nAlso: \"no personal names\" — \"VizH\" is an org/bot name cited in the drafts and explicitly allowed (\"Model and organization names the drafts already cite are fine\"). \"GrokBot\" also cited — I can cite it or not; the drafts say \"Original author: GrokBot\". I'll mention \"started by the Maker's Orchestrator\" or just omit. I'll avoid GrokBot to be safe? It's allowed, but not needed. Actually citing provenance is nice: \"Started 2026-10-28 by the Orchestrator of the Plumbline job ...\". Good.\n\nAlso avoid \"his\" pronoun referencing the Owner? The drafts use \"he\". Using \"he\" is fine (not a name). But G7 doesn't forbid pronouns. Still, \"the Owner\" is cleaner. I'll mostly use \"the Owner\".\n\nNow the writing.\n\nDraft:\n\n```\n# Running a job as Orchestrator: a field guide\n\nStatus: **non-normative**. This is a working document of practice, not doctrine, not a standard and not part of any custody record. Where it disagrees with the doctrine, the doctrine wins.\n\nStarted 2026-10-28 by the Orchestrator of the Plumbline job `2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps`, which closed accepted that morning, and revised the same day with a review from VizH, the Architect on the evaluatordriven custody jobs v0.9.6 and v0.9.7, and with three Owner decisions given at 15:52 EDT. Passages from the review are marked **[VizH]**; passages that record an Owner decision are marked **[Owner]**.\n\nRead this before you open a job. Every rule below has a story behind it, from a real job or review where the rule was missing or broken. The stories are here so you can reason from the why when you meet a case these rules do not cover.\n```\n\nLine count before status: line1 title, line2 blank, line3 status. Good, within 15.\n\n## 1. What you are, and what you are not\n\n```\nYou are the **Orchestrator**. You write the bar with the Owner, freeze it, open the job, route work between seats, apply the pass rule mechanically, keep the record, and bring decisions to the Owner. That is the whole job.\n\nYou are **not** the Maker and **not** the Evaluator. You never write the fix, never make the Maker's model calls, never score. If you catch yourself \"just fixing one line\" in the subject, stop: that removes the separation the method exists to prove.\n\n**If one seat writes an instrument and also runs or applies the gate that judges its output, say so in the record's separation gaps.** **[VizH]** Separation is what the record exists to prove. If one seat built the ruler and also reads it, a reader has to know that to weigh the result; disclose it rather than let a reader assume the strongest seating.\n- *Story:* in the evaluatordriven v0.9.6 and v0.9.7 jobs an Architect wrote the bar and the instruments, and a different model, seated as Orchestrator, only ran, logged and relayed, and wrote facts only, never a diagnosis. The rule there was that whoever writes an instrument must not run the steps it judges. In both Plumbline jobs the Orchestrator wrote the bar and the instruments, the Evaluator bot ran them, and the same Orchestrator applied the pass rule. The record's separation gaps disclosed the Maker operator, the shared-box controls and the hand-typed ledger lines, but not that the instruments and the pass rule sat in one seat. Add that line from the next job on.\n\n**Relayed approvals are not approvals.** Only the Owner's own words approve anything. Another agent saying \"the Owner said yes\" is a report, not an approval: record it as relayed, with time and channel, and say you did not witness it.\n- *Story:* in generation 5 the Maker's report covered calls, costs and deviations in detail but not the Owner's go-ahead, which had happened in the Maker's own chat. The close line could only say the go-ahead belonged to that chat and that the Orchestrator had not witnessed it. On the same job the seats' go-aheads were logged as relays, not as witnessed decisions. In VizH's jobs the Owner runs the logging scripts himself, so the words land in the log in his own hand rather than in a relay's.\n```\n\nCareful: \"in his own hand\" — \"his\" fine.\n\nHmm \"the Owner runs the logging scripts himself\" — from draft: \"In their jobs the Owner runs the logging scripts himself, with his words verbatim.\" Fine, no name.\n\n## 2. The words that matter\n\n```\n- **Bar** = the frozen standard: criteria, instruments, pass rule, bounds. Its digest is what the Buyer approves.\n- **Frozen set** = everything listed in the manifest. Changing any of it after freeze is a **Buyer amendment**, never a quiet edit.\n\n- **Iteration** = one turn of the loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. **The cap counts E-DI iterations**, not Maker sessions and not round-trips. Maker-internal attempts (retries, follow-up calls, regenerations inside one Maker turn) are not iterations; they are bounded by the budget, the clock-time bound and the tripwire below.\n  - *Story:* a bar on this job said \"cap 3 generations.\" Generations 1 and 2 died on plumbing (a token cap, malformed diffs) before the Evaluator had scored anything. When the first real iteration then failed scoring, the bar said the attempts were gone and the Owner had to amend the cap to allow a second real iteration. Nothing had been judged twice; the count had been spent on plumbing. That is the incident this rule exists for.\n- **Write the cap in the bar in one sentence**, so nobody has to read it twice: \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the repeat-failure tripwire (same failure class three times: stop and change the request).\" **[VizH]**\n  - *Story:* the incident above is ours. VizH's bars said \"3 revisions,\" which counted Maker sessions and round-trips, and their loop ran out of attempts for plumbing reasons before the Evaluator had scored anything. Two teams, the same misreading, independently.\n- **The repeat-failure tripwire: when the same failure class comes up a third time, stop and change the request.** **[Owner]** Exactly three: not two, not five. Log every Maker attempt with its failure class (`finish_reason` length with empty content, a diff or an extraction that will not apply, the same test failing, a provider refusal, and so on). The third occurrence of one class ends the retries of that request: the Maker changes the request (a smaller task, a split, a different delivery contract) or reports back. If the change touches the frozen set, it is an amendment for the Owner. A different failure class starts its own count.\n  - The tripwire does not cap spend; the budget does that. It bounds retries of one kind of failure, so a broken delivery path shows up while there is still budget to fix it.\n  - *Story:* in VizH's v0.9.6 job, 9 of 10 Maker attempts failed at \"the diff does not apply,\" with hunk counts off by one to three. A limit on attempts is what made it visible that the transport, not the model, was failing. Unlimited retries would have spent the budget on it.\n  - *Ours:* generation 2 returned four corrupt diffs and none applied. The contract was changed to whole files after one round, but nothing in the rules forced that. The `finish_reason` length rule in section 4 is the same idea applied on the first occurrence, because an identical retry cannot come out differently.\n```\n\nGood — the tripwire story.\n\n## 3. Before you freeze: the checklist\n\nItem 1: no throttling.\n\n```\n1. **No throttling, anywhere.** No reasoning limit, and max output tokens set to the endpoint maximum (look it up on the provider's endpoints page and save a snapshot). This applies to the Maker and to any model inside an Evaluator instrument.\n   - *Story:* Maker generation 1 ran under a 24k token cap; the model spent 19.9k tokens thinking and returned nothing usable.\n   - *Story:* an Evaluator instrument was frozen with a max of 800 tokens. All three scoring calls spent about 798 tokens reasoning and returned empty, the batch was void, and the Owner had to approve an amendment. The Owner's reading of it was that the instrument had been starved.\n2. **Size each task so the model can finish inside the maximum, and log `finish_reason` on every call.** **[VizH]** Removing your own cap is necessary, not sufficient: it removes the ceiling you set, not the endpoint's. A task too big for one reply burns the whole allowance on reasoning and returns no content, at full price. Split the work into calls that fit with room to spare, and have the harness log `finish_reason` with the reasoning and content token counts for every call, so a starved call shows up in the log instead of having to be guessed at.\n   - *Story:* in VizH's jobs one call asked for 12 records in a single reply and failed five times with `finish_reason` length at 131,072 tokens, almost all reasoning and no content, at about $0.59 a call. Batching fixed it: four calls of three records each.\n   - *Ours:* the guarded harness logs the finish reason and the token split already (one iteration came back `stop` at 21,753 of 26,185 tokens). Keep it that way, and make the sizing part of the bar rather than luck.\n3. **A live test of every model-calling instrument is a freeze gate.** One real call per instrument at the exact frozen settings (model, provider pin, max tokens, prompt template), with the request, reply, `finish_reason` and cost saved to a file whose digest is cited on the checklist. No attached output, no freeze. Dry runs and mocks do not catch a starved model.\n   - *Story:* VizH's team dry-ran the deterministic instruments (64 of 64 as expected) and never ran the records splitter live; five paid calls failed after the freeze. Ours was the 800-token instrument above. The job that did it right live-tested both pinned providers before freeze and committed the output under the frozen set.\n```\n\nItem 4: whole files + the command that writes them.\n\n```\n4. **Write the Maker's output contract into the bar, as whole files between markers, and name the command that writes them.** Models write whole files reliably and unified diffs badly.\n   - *Story:* generation 2 returned four complete-but-corrupt diffs and none applied; generation 3 delivered whole files through `extract_files.py` and passed the accept rule on the second call. The bar had said \"patch,\" which left the intent to interpretation. **[VizH]** 9 of 10 diff attempts failed to apply in v0.9.6 for the same reason, and their team dropped its plan to patch the diff parser.\n   - *Story (the command):* the first Maker handoff of a later job went from `extract_files.py --check`, which writes nothing, straight to \"commit\". The Maker noticed and ran the same command without `--check`, which wrote the reply verbatim, and committed that. Nothing was lost, but no handoff should leave the Maker to guess: **every handoff step that produces bytes names the command that writes them.**\n```\n\nItem 5: time bound as clock time + story.\nItem 6: approvals against clock and decision cap + story.\nItem 7: spend in harness, shared key, spend guard, upper bound + story.\nItem 8: pre-read optional on approval sheet + story.\nItem 9: malformed input probe + story.\nItem 10: handoffs checked against standing rules + story. — Hmm, this is more a \"running the loop\" thing but it's a pre-freeze/pre-dispatch check. I'll put it in section 4 (Maker turn) as \"check the handoff against standing rules\" with the story. Actually it's about handoffs generally. Put in section 4.\n\nItem 11: log every Owner decision as it happens + story — put in section 5 (records) or here. Draft puts it in checklist item 10. I'll put it in section 5 \"Records\" as a rule with the story. Fine either way.\n\nLet me write item 5-9:\n\n```\n5. **State the time bound as a clock time**, for example \"ends 2026-10-28 13:15 EDT,\" never \"under 10 hours.\" Derive every cutoff from it in the handoffs and show the arithmetic.\n   - *Story:* a call-start cutoff (02:15) and the job's end (stated as 03:15, but strictly 05:44 from open) were mixed up, and nobody could tell which number was the real limit.\n6. **Plan the approval checkpoints against the clock, and against any cap on Owner decisions.** Every Maker or Evaluator model run needs the Owner's yes. If the Owner is asleep, the clock keeps running.\n   - *Story:* generation 3 waited overnight for a yes and the job overran its time bound before the call started, which took an amendment. Either schedule runs when the Owner is around, or get the yeses up front for a defined sequence.\n   - *Story (decision cap):* in VizH's jobs the bar capped Owner decisions at 10 and the job used 12: ten were logged by event, two came by chat, and one of the ten could not be tied to any event. A standing ruling for a defined sequence (\"proceed, uncapped, three per session\") saved them several asks. Ours was an up-front approval of the first three iterations.\n7. **Enforce spend in the harness, not in prose.** A ceiling written in a handoff is procedural; nothing stops a runaway call. Put per-call and per-batch caps in the tool. **[Owner]** The key stays **shared**, behind the harness's pre-call **spend guard**, and key-usage reconciliation is reported as an **upper bound**.\n   - What the guard covers: it refuses a call before anything is sent when the job's spend plus the call's worst case plus a small reserve would reach the bound. Spend is the larger of the job's own ledger and the key's usage change since the job's baseline; the worst case is prompt bytes times the input price plus max tokens times the output price; reserves for unsettled calls count as spent; only priced model and provider pairs can be called; it refuses when key usage cannot be read.\n   - What it does not cover, which the record must say: spend that bypasses the guard (another tool or job on the same key) is counted after the fact, not stopped; other spend on the key counts against the job, so the key-usage figure is an upper bound and not exact, and a busy key can refuse a legitimate call; key usage lags, so read it at least two to three minutes after the last call; and with no provider-side ceiling there is no backstop if the guard has a bug. The Owner weighed a per-job key with a provider-enforced limit, which would make the ceiling real and reconciliation exact, and chose the shared key instead; this guide records that decision and does not reopen it.\n   - *Story:* VizH's jobs shared a key with other work, the job ledger and the key's total never reconciled, and the key's own limit stopped a call partway through. Ours ran the same way and matched to the last digit — $0.014041 on the ledger, the same as the key-usage change — which was luck, not a guarantee: nothing else happened to touch the key during the job.\n8. **The cross-family pre-read is optional, and it is decided per job on the approval sheet.** **[Owner]** Put it on every approval sheet as a numbered item with your recommendation: yes for a new kind of bar or new instruments, usually no for a small repeat of a known shape. If the Owner says yes, a reviewer from a model family not already in the job reads the bar, the runbook and the instruments before freeze and lists what is unclear or unfair; every finding is fixed or justified in a file committed with the job, and the rounds repeat until one adds nothing new. It catches wording and gate problems. It cannot catch a starved model, so it never replaces the live test above.\n   - *Story:* in VizH's jobs successive pre-read rounds returned 35, then 38, then 4, then 3, then 0 findings, and the Architect fixed or justified each one.\n9. **Show every checker a malformed input before you trust it on a good one.** **[VizH]** Any instrument that settles a question (a checker, a tiebreak, a schema validator, a verifier) gets records with missing fields, malformed fields and wrong types first, and must fail them the way the specification says.\n   - *Story:* two adjudicators split on a question in v0.9.6; the tiebreak instrument did not implement the specification's rule for missing and malformed fields, and it settled the split the wrong way.\n   - *Ours:* the custody-times job's item 29, one planted case per failure type of the verifier, is this rule written as a bar item.\n```\n\nHmm: \"$0.014041 on the ledger, the same as the key-usage change (0.303493375 → 0.317534503)\". Should I include the numbers? They're fine (no key material). Difference: 0.317534503 - 0.303493375 = 0.014041128. The draft says \"$0.014041 on the ledger and the same as the key-usage change\". I'll keep it simple without the full numbers to avoid clutter. Actually numbers add credibility. Keep \"$0.014041\" only.\n\nFreeze gate checklist — the drafts have one. It's useful and includes the anchors again (live test, freeze gate, command that writes, standing rules...). Including it adds ~1KB. I'll include a compact version, maybe 8 boxes.\n\n```\n**Freeze gate: tick every box before you freeze**\n- [ ] Every model-calling instrument run live at the frozen settings, with the output attached and its digest cited (item 3).\n- [ ] Each task sized to finish inside the model's maximum, and `finish_reason` logged on every call (item 2).\n- [ ] No throttling: max tokens at the endpoint maximum from a saved snapshot, and no reasoning limit (item 1).\n- [ ] Maker delivers whole files between markers; the runbook names the command that writes them; the extracted paths are checked against the allow-list.\n- [ ] Cap written in one sentence as \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the repeat-failure tripwire\" (section 2).\n- [ ] Spend enforced in the harness: per-call and per-batch caps plus the pre-call guard on the shared key, its baseline set and its refusal tested before freeze; the record will call key-usage reconciliation an upper bound (item 7).\n- [ ] Cross-family pre-read decided on the approval sheet (item 8).\n- [ ] Every checker shown a malformed input and seen to fail it (item 9).\n- [ ] Owner decisions planned against the clock and any decision cap, and logged with time, channel, words and whether witnessed or relayed.\n- [ ] Instruments and redacted logs planned to be committed beside the work, and logged files planned write-once by content (section 5).\n- [ ] If one seat writes instruments and runs or applies the gate, a separation-gap line is drafted (section 1).\n```\n\n## 4. Running the loop\n\nMaker turn:\n\n```\n- **Hand off with exact preconditions**: branch head, ledger digest, prompt file digest, harness digest, log entry count, clock cutoff. If any does not match, the Maker stops. That is a feature.\n- **Relay the shortfall unedited.** The Evaluator's findings come to you filtered by script; give them to the Maker as the instrument's reasons and the judge's reply, without rewriting, summarizing or diagnosing. **Redact anything from held-out controls**: the Maker must never see them.\n- **Verify the Maker's report yourself** (or have your worker do it): re-extract the files from the raw reply and confirm they are byte-identical to the commit, check the paths against the allow-list, re-run the tests, reconcile spend with key usage. Trust, then verify.\n- **When a Maker attempt breaks the tests, run the same tests at the unchanged base commit, in the same environment, before you blame anyone.** If the base passes, the break is in the Maker's files; if the base fails too, it is your setup. Record the base run as a deviation, outside the repo, so it is on file but cannot be mistaken for Maker evidence.\n  - *Story:* in generation 5, call 2's files crashed the suite with a missing-file error. It was the first run under a new temp root, so the environment was a real suspect. The Maker ran one extra test at the unchanged base in a throwaway worktree with the same environment, and 112 of 112 passed. That one run settled it: the crash was the Maker's, the failure text went back to the model, and call 4 was accepted.\n- **Treat `finish_reason` length with empty content as its own failure class: stop at the first one, resize the request, and do not retry it as is.** This is the one class that does not wait for the tripwire's third time, because an identical retry cannot come out differently: the same request at the same maximum gives the same result and the same bill. The changed request is a new Maker attempt, not an iteration. If the change alters the delivery contract frozen in the bar, it is an amendment.\n  - *Story:* the 12-record call above was retried five times at 131,072 tokens, about $3 before anyone changed the request. Ours carried the same signature twice: generation 1 under a 24k cap and the 800-token instrument batch, both reasoning with no content. Read it as \"resize,\" not \"retry.\"\n- **\"The tests pass\" tells you the files fit the harness, not how the finding was fixed. Read the diff and say what kind of fix it is** — not judging it, that is the Evaluator's job, but writing it plainly in the generation close line so the Owner is not surprised.\n  - *Story:* iteration 2 failed on hand-typed custody times in a repository fixture with no ledger line behind them. The next generation added four ledger events to that fixture carrying the same hand-typed times, and relaxed the emitter's cross-checks so the new events would agree; the emitter still neither refuses nor flags hand-typed times. The allow-list included the fixtures folder, so it was a legal move and the easiest one. Whether it met the bar was the Evaluator's call, but \"is a fixture-only fix acceptable?\" should have been settled in the prompt. Do instead: if the fix has to be in the code, leave fixtures off the allow-list, or say in the prompt that changing a fixture to make a finding go away does not count.\n- **Ask the Maker to quote the Owner's go-ahead word for word in its report.** You need it for the close line and the record, and you did not see it happen, because it happened in the Maker's chat. (Story: section 1.)\n- **Diff each filled handoff against the previous one before you dispatch it.** A template copied forward carries its old words.\n  - *Story:* a batch-4 template still said the batch-3 directory did not exist yet, in a precondition. It was harmless, but a newcomer Evaluator could have checked the wrong folder.\n- **Check every handoff against the standing rules before you send it.** A handoff written by a helper can drift quietly.\n  - *Story:* a first iteration's Maker handoff, written by a helper, capped Maker retries at one and committed as the Owner. Both contradict standing rules: the cap counts E-DI iterations, and the Maker is the author with the Owner as committer. Both were caught and overridden in the relay. Check the profile rules explicitly — no throttling, the retry policy, git identity, and no `/tmp`.\n```\n\nHmm — \"no `/tmp`\" appears here; fine.\n\nEvaluator turn:\n\n```\n- **Give the Evaluator a writable copy of the controls**, and check it against the manifest before and after.\n  - *Story:* the copy was made read-only to \"protect\" it. The scoring harness copies fixtures with their permissions, so the subject's emitter could not write its output and five checks failed for environmental reasons. The batch was void.\n- **Read key usage at least two to three minutes after the last call**, or the last call's cost will not show yet.\n  - *Story:* one batch waited 156 seconds after the last call, and the after-read reconciled to the cent — the fix for an earlier under-count.\n- **When results look strange, ask \"is this the subject or my setup?\" before applying the pass rule.** A void batch is recorded as void with its causes; it is not a score.\n- **Even in a void batch, look for real signal.** In the void batch above, two checks failed on a fixture the setup bug did not touch; that was a true result and it held in the next batch.\n- **The control order rotates per batch, so read which call is the subject from the batch's own order.** Do not assume a position.\n  - *Story:* in batch 4 a control file ran in the order [subject, control one, control two], so the subject was the first call, not the second as in an earlier batch. The verdict, the evidence copy and the close inputs all take the subject's call name from the batch's own list.\n```\n\nPass rule:\n\n```\n**Applying the pass rule**\n- Mechanical: every dimension must pass. Report what failed, quote the instrument's reason in plain words, and say what it means for the work.\n- At the cap the state is `needs owner`. Bring the Owner the options (amend, reject) with your recommendation and the clock arithmetic for each.\n```\n\n## 5. Records\n\n```\n- The job record lives **in the product repo** beside the code, in the job folder the doctrine names. Not in scratch.\n- **Commit the instruments and the redacted logs beside the work, not just the record.** **[VizH]** The doctrine says the record and the instrument that accepted the work are committed beside it, not in local scratch. The job folder should hold the scoring scripts, rubrics, harness, the redacted request and reply logs, and the digest of the full log.\n  - *Story:* VizH's v0.9.7 repo carried the record but not the job log, the 130 evidence files or the acceptance instruments. A public check of the clone read not-checkable on two requirements, and the job had called that expected. Committing the redacted logs with the full log's digest is the fix they pointed to.\n- The ledger is append-only. Every amendment, generation open and close, and evaluation verdict gets a line; record the ledger digest after each append and pin it in the next handoff. The ledger tool could not yet write generation, evaluation or amendment lines, so they were hand-appended, and the follow-up to add those commands is carried forward.\n- **Keep logged files write-once, by content.** Before an event cites a file, copy it to an evidence file named with the first eight hex digits of its digest, and cite the copy. A correction is a new file and a new event, never an overwrite. At close, run the log's file check and say which cited files no longer match and why.\n  - *Story:* while drafting the custody-times job we found the emitter rewrites evidence logs in place, filling send and receive times from the server's clock, and that running the tests changes two committed fixture logs. Content-named, write-once evidence makes that change visible as a mismatch instead of a silent edit. **[VizH]** VizH's jobs work to the same rule; the review gives it without a specific incident of its own.\n- Post-freeze amendments go **beside** the frozen set, at the job root, never inside the frozen folder, so the bar digest stays valid. Each amendment records the Owner's words, with time, channel and whether you witnessed it.\n- **Log every Owner decision as it happens, in the same form**: time with zone, channel or message id, the words, and witnessed or relayed. A cap on decisions is only as good as the log behind it. (Story: the decision cap of 10 against 12 used, section 3 item 6.)\n- Model-judge logs are committed **redacted**, requests and replies, since replies can quote control code, with the full log's digest alongside. The full log stays outside the repo.\n- The provider key is referenced by path only. Scan for it before every push.\n- Git identity: the Maker is the author of its commits and the Owner is the committer, with the Owner's no-reply address. No handoff may quietly change that.\n```\n\nHmm \"the Owner's no-reply address\" — fine, no email.\n\n## 6. The box under you\n\n```\n- **Your computer can reboot under you.** On this job it rebooted at about 08:36 EDT during Maker generation 4, call 3: the call died with no exit file and no log entry, the temp area was wiped, the scratch worktree vanished, and 44 committed files in an unrelated job folder showed as deleted. What to do: do not panic and do not re-run anything yet; check what is actually damaged against git; restore committed files from git, never from memory; record the lost call as lost, with what you know (start time, likely cost from key usage) and never invent a log entry for it; resume from the last good state.\n- **No job work in `/tmp`.** It is wiped on reboot. **[Owner]**\n  - *Story:* the handoffs built the test venv under `/tmp`, the Maker ran its calls from wrapper scripts there, and rebuild checks wrote their output there. The reboot took all of it and the Maker had to rebuild before it could resume.\n  - Do instead: keep venvs, wrapper scripts, scratch worktrees and intermediate files under a job work root that survives reboots, and write that path into every handoff's names section. Commit evidence as each step finishes, so a crash cannot take it.\n- **Moving your own paths is not enough; set the tools' temp directory too.** Python's tempfile, mktemp and most test suites write to `/tmp` on their own, whatever your handoff names.\n  - *Story:* after the reboot, the next handoff moved every named path to a work root, but nobody told the tests where to put their temp files. The Maker caught it in pre-checks, created a job-local temp folder, exported the temp-directory variable to it, and only then ran anything; one test script alone left 32 temp folders there. At close, list and clean those folders, and the old leftovers too.\n- **Every environment dependency outside the repo goes into the handoff preconditions, with a script to restore it.**\n  - *Story:* the test suite reads the doctrine corpus through `tools/pin.json` at a path outside the repository — on that box a root-owned symlink to a checkout. No handoff mentioned it. The reboot wiped it and the tests could not run until the cause was found and the symlink recreated, after checking the corpus files against the pinned hashes.\n- **Record exact tool and venv versions at job open.**\n  - *Story:* the test venv was rebuilt after the reboot and nothing written at job open said what the original held, so \"same as before\" could only be assumed. The identical test output was the only evidence that the rebuild matched. Commit a version record at open and diff a rebuilt environment against it.\n```\n\n## 7. Working with the Owner\n\n```\n- The Owner decides; you recommend. Lead with the answer, give your recommendation, and offer real options. Show the clock arithmetic when time matters.\n- **When the Owner gives a principle, apply it everywhere it fits**, not only where it was said, and write it down here.\n- **Own your mistakes plainly.** \"I froze an 800-token cap\" beats explaining.\n- Push only on the Owner's authority, and cite the message.\n- **If you are checking a live site, send an HTML accept header.** A bare request sends a wildcard and can miss a script injected only for HTML requests.\n  - *Story:* in VizH's jobs a bare request missed a script that the CDN injected only into HTML requests. This guide has no story of its own for it yet.\n```\n\n## 8. Preparing the close, and after the merge\n\n```\n- **Take every time floor from the log itself, never from a summary of it.**\n  - *Story:* the floor was first set to 14:23:43Z from batch notes. The dry-run record showed a control finishing at 14:24:26Z, later than the \"last\" time. The harness log said the last reply was received at 14:24:43Z and fetched at 14:24:48Z. The floor moved to 14:24:49Z. With the wrong floor, a decision time in that one minute would have dated the Owner's yes before the check it approved.\n- **Have the close ready before the Owner decides**, so the yes turns into a record in seconds.\n  - *Story:* disposition came at 10:44:00 EDT; the close script committed 28 seconds later, the record checked clean, and the pull request merged 57 seconds after that. Nothing was improvised at the moment that counted, because every surprise had already happened in a dry run on a scratch branch.\n- **Dry-run the whole close, commit included, on a scratch branch, and test both a given and a declined disposition.**\n  - *Story:* the dry run is what caught a conformance failure: the instrument's runner was the same script as the evaluator it ran. A script evaluator's script must not be the instrument's runner; the runner is the written procedure that ran the evaluators.\n- **After the merge the job branch is finished.** Later changes to the job record go on a new branch cut from the merge commit.\n  - *Story:* this guide's final sync came after the pull request was merged; it went on a small record branch cut from main and touched only this file. Commits added to a merged job branch would have been stranded off main.\n- **A merge changes the next job's starting point. Re-read the pins before drafting.** After the merge, main's emitter was the fixed one, so the next job's record no longer needed the old limitation disclosure. Check, do not copy, the previous sheet's pins.\n```\n\n## 9. The three Owner decisions of 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT, in one place\n\n```\n- **Tripwire.** At exactly three occurrences of the same failure class, stop and change the request (section 2).\n- **Shared key.** The shared key stays, behind the harness's pre-call spend guard, and key-usage reconciliation is reported as an upper bound; the guide does not reopen the per-job key (section 3, item 7).\n- **Pre-read.** The cross-family pre-read is optional and is decided per job on the approval sheet (section 3, item 8).\n```\n\n## 10. Carried forward\n\n```\n- **Fixture-only fixes.** Is changing a fixture an acceptable fix for a finding about that fixture? Settle it in the bar and the allow-list before the Maker's first turn.\n- **The x-plumbline sidecar.** The emitter of the last job could not carry extra record fields, so spend and a limitation note went into a sidecar file beside the record. Either adopt the sidecar as the rule or add a pass-through.\n- **Only the final iteration got a run record.** Iterations 1 and 2 live in their verdict files and the ledger. Decide whether every iteration gets one.\n- **Follow-up job:** make the emitter refuse or flag hand-entered times, add ledger commands for generation, evaluation, amendment and disposition-at-decision-time, fixture modes, and spend caps enforced in the harness.\n- **Skill update.** The rules from sections 2 to 5 are drafted for the skill and not applied; the Owner decides.\n```\n\nGood, the heading \"## 10. Carried forward\" matches `^#{1,6} .*carried forward` (case-insensitive presumably; if case-sensitive, \"Carried forward\" vs regex \"carried forward\"... The regex given is `^#{1,6} .*carried forward` — if applied case-insensitively (likely, since matching works on normalized lower-case text), fine. Since matching works on normalized text (lowercased), the heading becomes \"## 10. carried forward\" and the r